{
  "name": "happy",
  "integration": "opnsense",
  "description": "One firewall plus five discovered hosts across ARP, NDP, and both Kea lease pools. Row shapes are copied from the OPNsense collectors themselves: scripts/interfaces/list_arp.py emits {mac, ip, intf, expired, expires, permanent, type, manufacturer, hostname} and the controller adds intf_description; list_ndp.py emits {mac, ip, intf, manufacturer}; scripts/kea/get_kea_leases.py emits {address, prefix_len, type, hwaddr, duid, client_id, iaid, valid_lifetime, expire, hostname, state, if, if_descr, is_reserved} and Kea/Api/LeasesController.php adds if_name and mac_info before wrapping everything as {total, rowCount, current, rows}. Proves: the ARP/NDP/lease views of one MAC (aa:bb:cc:00:11:01) collapse into a single asset carrying both its v4 and its v6 address and its DHCP hostname; the firewall's own permanent ARP entry and its own interface MAC are excluded so its addresses do not land on a second asset; a link-local-only NDP neighbor still imports on its MAC alone; loopback on lo0 and fe80::/10 everywhere are dropped from interfaces; and the dnsmasq backend answering 404 while Kea answers is not an error. no_mac_in_id is skipped for every opnsense scenario: OPNsense issues no device identifier of any kind, the MAC is the key of all three tables, and the id is deliberately paired with no-id-match no-id-break so it never drives a merge.",
  "kwargs": {
    "url": "$BASE",
    "api_key": "fixture-key",
    "api_secret": "fixture-secret"
  },
